                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-why-53ft",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Why are 53 ft Dry Van Trailers so Common?",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "why-53ft"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-why-53ft-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "The reason the 53 ft dry van trailer became the industry standard is simple: efficiency. Long-haul trucking companies and independent operators need equipment that maximizes capacity while staying compliant with federal highway regulations. The 53-foot design does exactly that.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-why-53ft-2",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "<strong>Advantages of a 53 ft dry van trailer include:</strong>",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "features-why-53ft-3",
                  "type": "list",
                  "props": {
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "columns": 2,
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "label": "Higher Cargo Volume",
                        "desc": "More space means you can haul more freight in a single trip."
                      },
                      {
                        "label": "Better Revenue Potential",
                        "desc": "With more pallet capacity, each load can generate higher income."
                      },
                      {
                        "label": "Versatility Across Industries",
                        "desc": "Retail goods, packaged foods, furniture, electronics, and manufacturing supplies all move in dry vans."
                      },
                      {
                        "label": "Wide Industry Acceptance",
                        "desc": "Most shippers and distribution centers are built to accommodate 53-foot trailers."
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-why-53ft-4",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Because of these advantages, many businesses searching for a 53 ft dry van trailer for sale are looking for equipment that will remain useful for years to come.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "fig-1",
                  "type": "image",
                  "props": {
                    "width": "full",
                    "image": {
                      "src": "/img/blog/standard-size-of-a-dry-van-trailer/1.jpg",
                      "width": 1000,
                      "height": 667,
                      "alt": "Sun State service bay with a dry van trailer and fire trucks in for maintenance"
                    }
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-48ft",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "When 48 ft Dry Van Trailers Make Sense",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "48ft"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-48ft-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Before the 53-foot model became dominant, the 48 ft dry van trailer was considered the industry standard. While they're less common today, 48 ft dry van trailers for sale still serve an important role in certain operations.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-48ft-2",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "<strong>A 48 ft dry van trailer typically provides:</strong>",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "list-48ft-3",
                  "type": "prose-list",
                  "props": {
                    "ordered": false,
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "text": "Cargo space for about 24 pallets"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Easier maneuverability in tight urban areas"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Slightly lighter overall weight compared to longer trailers"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-48ft-4",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Many regional fleets and businesses operating in cities prefer this size because it can be easier to handle in tighter loading docks or congested routes. If your operation focuses on regional deliveries rather than long-haul freight, a 48 ft dry van trailer can still be a practical and dependable option.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-28ft",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "The Role of 28 ft Dry Van Trailers",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "28ft"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-28ft-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "At the other end of the spectrum is the 28 ft dry van trailer, often called a pup trailer. You'll most commonly see these in less-than-truckload (LTL) freight operations where flexibility and efficiency are essential.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-28ft-2",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "<strong>Typical uses for a 28 ft dry van trailer for sale include:</strong>",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "list-28ft-3",
                  "type": "prose-list",
                  "props": {
                    "ordered": false,
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "text": "LTL shipping operations"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Double trailer configurations"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Short regional deliveries"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Hub-and-spoke distribution networks"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-28ft-4",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Because two 28-foot trailers can be pulled in tandem in certain situations, they offer flexibility that larger trailers cannot. For carriers that handle multiple small shipments instead of full truckloads, 28 ft dry van trailers can be an important part of a fleet.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "fig-2",
                  "type": "image",
                  "props": {
                    "width": "full",
                    "image": {
                      "src": "/img/blog/standard-size-of-a-dry-van-trailer/2.jpg",
                      "width": 1000,
                      "height": 667,
                      "alt": "Sun State service bay doors with vehicles in for maintenance"
                    }
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-choosing-size",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "How to Choose the Right Dry Van Trailer Size",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "choosing-size"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-choosing-size-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "When deciding between trailer sizes, it's important to look beyond the specs and think about your day-to-day operation.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-choosing-size-2",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "<strong>Here are a few factors to consider:</strong>",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "features-choosing-size-3",
                  "type": "list",
                  "props": {
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "columns": 2,
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "label": "Freight Type",
                        "desc": "Different cargo types require different trailer capacities. If your loads typically fill an entire trailer, a 53 ft dry van trailer could be the best choice."
                      },
                      {
                        "label": "Route Type",
                        "desc": "If you're primarily driving urban routes, you may prefer a more maneuverable option like a 48 ft trailer, while 53 ft models are better for long highway routes."
                      },
                      {
                        "label": "Business Growth",
                        "desc": "Choosing a trailer size that supports future contracts can help avoid costly upgrades later."
                      },
                      {
                        "label": "Budget and Availability",
                        "desc": "Depending on your needs, used trailer options may be a practical way to expand capacity without high upfront costs."
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-choosing-size-4",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "At Sun State Trailers, we work with customers to evaluate these factors so they can invest in equipment that truly supports their business.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-at-a-glance",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Differences Between 53ft, 48ft, and 28ft Trailers At A Glance",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "at-a-glance"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "compare-at-a-glance-1",
                  "type": "compare-table",
                  "props": {
                    "headA": "Feature",
                    "headB": "53 ft",
                    "headC": "48 ft",
                    "headD": "28 ft",
                    "rows": [
                      {
